{"causes":["The Venezuelan War of Independence from Spain (1810-1823) led to the country's founding on July 5, 1845.","The discovery of oil in the early 20th century transformed Venezuela into one of the wealthiest countries in South America.","The global economic crisis of the 2008 financial crisis had a significant impact on Venezuela's economy and contributed to its current economic struggles."],"effects":["Venezuela's oil exports have been a major contributor to the global economy, fueling economic growth in countries around the world.","The country's authoritarian government has led to widespread human rights abuses and democratic backsliding globally.","The ongoing economic crisis in Venezuela has had far-reaching consequences for its people, including high levels of poverty and migration."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Hugo Chávez","sourceName":"Hugo Chávez Maduro Administration","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Venezuela","targetName":"Government of Venezuela","context":"Chávez's presidency was marked by widespread corruption and authoritarianism."},{"sourceSlug":"Simón Bolívar","sourceName":"South American Liberation Movement","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Venezuela","targetName":"Government of Venezuela","context":"Bolívar's vision for a unified South America inspired the Venezuelan War of Independence."},{"sourceSlug":"OPEC","sourceName":"Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"Venezuela","targetName":"Government of Venezuela","context":"As a member of OPEC, Venezuela plays a key role in global oil production and pricing."},{"sourceSlug":"US Embassy in Caracas","sourceName":"United States Department of State","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Venezuela","targetName":"Government of Venezuela","context":"The US Embassy in Caracas has been the site of numerous protests and demonstrations against the Maduro administration."},{"sourceSlug":"Andrés Bello","sourceName":"Simón Bolívar's presidential campaign","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Venezuela","targetName":"Government of Venezuela","context":"Bello's ideas about education and social reform inspired the Venezuelan War of Independence."},{"sourceSlug":"Juan Bautista Muñoz","sourceName":"South American Liberation Movement","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Venezuela","targetName":"Government of Venezuela","context":"Muñoz's military campaigns contributed to the Venezuelan War of Independence."}],"places":[{"name":"Caracas","role":"Capital city"},{"name":"Maracaibo","role":"Second-largest city"},{"name":"Orinoco Delta","role":"Major oil-producing region"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Venezuela","type":"Reference article","slug":"venezuela"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q717","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venezuela"],"tags":["country","Countries / Polities","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Venezuela is remembered as a landmark place of the Modern era, referenced in 340 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Served as a center of human activity and development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Venezuela (Bolivarian Republic of Venezuela) is a South American nation occupying the northern coast of the continent on the Caribbean Sea, bordered by Colombia to the west, Brazil to the south, and Guyana to the east. With an area of 916,445 km² and a population of approximately 32 million, Venezuela possesses some of the world's largest proven oil reserves (the largest by conventional crude estimates as of the early 21st century, largely in the Orinoco Belt) and a landscape of extraordinary natural diversity: the Andes in the west, the llanos (vast tropical grasslands) of the interior, the Amazon rainforest in the south, the Guiana Highlands (home to the world's highest waterfall, Angel Falls/Salto Ángel), and 2,800 km of Caribbean coastline.

Venezuela was colonised by Spain from the early 16th century, with the first permanent European settlement at Coro (1527). The indigenous population — comprising diverse peoples including the Carib, Arawak, and various Andean and Amazonian groups — was devastated by disease, enslavement, and displacement. Venezuela was among the first South American territories to declare independence from Spain: Francisco de Miranda's 1811 declaration and Simón Bolívar's subsequent military campaigns led to formal independence in 1821. Bolívar himself was born in Caracas (1783), making Venezuela the birthplace of the 'Liberator' whose military campaigns freed most of northern South America.

Venezuela's modern history has been dominated by oil — discovered in commercial quantities at Lake Maracaibo in 1914 and rapidly transforming the country's economy and politics. The 'sowing the oil' (sembrar el petróleo) dream — using oil revenues to diversify and develop the economy — was never fully realised, and Venezuela's political history has oscillated between democratic and authoritarian governments. The Bolivarian Revolution of Hugo Chávez (1999–2013) and its continuation under Nicolás Maduro produced a severe economic and humanitarian crisis in the 2010s–2020s, with the world's largest recorded hyperinflation and a massive emigration of over 7 million Venezuelans.","subjects":["South America","Venezuelan History","Modern Era","Latin America","Oil Politics","Contemporary Era","Independence Movements","Political History"],"frameworks":["WORLD_SYSTEMS","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Venezuela's historical significance lies in its complex and multifaceted impact on the global community, including its role as a major producer of oil and its contribution to democratic struggles globally.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
